import RequestProject.Epistemic.Trit

/-!
# Context erasure can reverse a direction

Every module in this development that insists on keeping a coordinate visible —
the layer vector behind a pooled square, the residual behind a projection, the
authority gate behind a promotion — is answering the same worry: what happens
when a distinction is dropped.  This module gives the sharpest finite instance
of that worry, the reversal of an association under aggregation.

Rates are compared *without division*, by cross-multiplication: `Better x y`
holds when `x.successes * y.total > y.successes * x.total`.  On positive totals
that is exactly the comparison of the two rates, and it keeps everything inside
`Nat` and decidable.

The witness is the kidney-stone contingency table reported by Julious and Mullee
(BMJ, 1994) for Simpson's phenomenon (Simpson, JRSS B, 1951).  Method A wins in
the smaller-stone stratum (81/87 against 234/270) and wins again in the
larger-stone stratum (192/263 against 55/80), yet after the strata are pooled
away Method B wins the margin (289/350 against 273/350):
`context_erasure_reverses_direction`.

`marginals_do_not_determine_conditional_direction` is the structural conclusion,
and it is stronger than the single witness: two studies with *identical*
marginals in both arms can disagree in every stratum, one favouring A throughout
and the other favouring B throughout.  So the aggregate carries no information
at all about the direction inside the retained contexts.

Nothing causal or clinical is claimed here; the counts are used only as an exact
countermodel to context erasure.
-/

